    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d568">

                <pb facs="0632" n="624" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg624"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">665.116/405: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-02-01T19:00:00+01:00">February 1, 1939—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 1—4:48 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. The list of quotas for American imports into Italy in 1939 has been received.
                    In general the quotas have been calculated upon the basis of article 8 included
                    in the temporary commercial agreement<anchor n="5" xml:id="d568-f1"/> and for
                    the purposes of such calculations 1934 has again been chosen as the
                    representative year.</p>
                <p>The quotas for 18 commodities are less than would result from a strict
                    application of the provisions of article 8 as was the case with similar items
                    last year. These reductions calculated on the basis of customs import valuations
                    in 1938 amount to approximately 52 million lire. In “compensation” quotas for 8
                    products have been increased by approximately 56 million lire. No quotas are
                    granted for pork fat, bacon and zinc since the domestic production of these
                    commodities has increased to such an extent as to render their importation
                    “almost unnecessary” during the current year.</p>
                <p>Full report and list of quotas by mail. Inform Commerce.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d568-f1" n="5" xml:id="d568-f1-content">
                <p>For text of temporary commercial arrangement between the United States and Italy,
                    signed December 16, 1937, see Department of State Executive Agreement Series No.
                    116, or 51 Stat 361. For correspondence, see <ref target="frus1937v02#pg_435"
                            ><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1937, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 435</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
